dho:c-02 a dho:Condition ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.12-1.2,
        dho:p-sn46.48-1.2 ;
    dho:condition dho:GoodFriendship ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SevenAwakeningFactors ;
    dho:relation dho:ForerunnerOf ;
    dho:wording "good friendship is the forerunner and harbinger of the arising of the seven awakening factors" .

dho:c-04 a dho:Condition ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.13-1.2 ;
    dho:condition dho:RationalAttention ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SevenAwakeningFactors ;
    dho:relation dho:ForerunnerOf ;
    dho:wording "rational application of mind is the forerunner and harbinger of the arising of the seven awakening factors" .

dho:c-g1 a dho:Condition ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in kāmarāga, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-3.1,
        dho:p-sn46.55-4.3 ;
    dho:condition dho:SensualLust ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ;
    dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:wording "your heart is overcome and mired in sensual desire and you don’t truly understand the escape from sensual desire that has arisen. At that time you don’t truly know or see what is good for yourself, good for another, or good for both." .

dho:c-g2 a dho:Condition ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in byāpāda, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-5.1,
        dho:p-sn46.55-6.3 ;
    dho:condition dho:IllWill ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ;
    dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:support dho:ExpandedAbbreviation ;
    dho:wording "yasmiṁ samaye byāpādapariyuṭṭhitena cetasā viharati byāpādaparetena, uppannassa ca byāpādassa nissaraṇaṁ yathābhūtaṁ nappajānāti, attatthampi tasmiṁ samaye yathābhūtaṁ na jānāti na passati" .

dho:c-g3 a dho:Condition ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in thinamiddha, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-7.1,
        dho:p-sn46.55-8.3 ;
    dho:condition dho:DullnessDrowsiness ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ;
    dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:support dho:ExpandedAbbreviation ;
    dho:wording "yasmiṁ samaye thinamiddhapariyuṭṭhitena cetasā viharati thinamiddhaparetena, uppannassa ca thinamiddhassa nissaraṇaṁ yathābhūtaṁ nappajānāti, attatthampi tasmiṁ samaye yathābhūtaṁ na jānāti na passati" .

dho:c-g4 a dho:Condition ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in uddhaccakukkucca, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-10.3,
        dho:p-sn46.55-9.1 ;
    dho:condition dho:RestlessnessRemorse ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ;
    dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:support dho:ExpandedAbbreviation ;
    dho:wording "yasmiṁ samaye uddhaccakukkuccapariyuṭṭhitena cetasā viharati uddhaccakukkuccaparetena, uppannassa ca uddhaccakukkuccassa nissaraṇaṁ yathābhūtaṁ nappajānāti, attatthampi tasmiṁ samaye yathābhūtaṁ na jānāti na passati" .

dho:c-g5 a dho:Condition ;
    skos:note "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in vicikicchā, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.55-11.1,
        dho:p-sn46.55-12.3 ;
    dho:condition dho:Doubt ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SeeingWhatIsGood ;
    dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:result dho:IsAbsent ;
    dho:wording "yasmiṁ samaye vicikicchāpariyuṭṭhitena cetasā viharati vicikicchāparetena, uppannāya ca vicikicchāya nissaraṇaṁ yathābhūtaṁ nappajānāti, attatthampi tasmiṁ samaye yathābhūtaṁ na jānāti na passati" .

dho:c-u3 a dho:Condition ;
    skos:note "SN 46.42 calls the factors 'treasures' (bojjhaṅgaratana) and says they appear with (pātubhāvā) a Realized One's appearance; SN 46.9 (c-u1) says they don't arise apart from it. Two claims, kept apart."@en ;
    dho:citedBy dho:p-sn46.42-2.1,
        dho:p-sn46.42-2.4 ;
    dho:condition dho:AppearanceOfRealizedOne ;
    dho:conditioned dho:SevenAwakeningFactors ;
    dho:relation dho:WhenThen ;
    dho:result dho:Arises ;
    dho:wording "When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear" .

